1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,236
Imagine you are in an interview, and the professor asks you,

2
00:00:04,256 --> 00:00:07,015
"What is your favorite theorem?"

3
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:09,207
Here is mine.

4
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:14,239
Take the primes five, thirteen, seventeen and twenty-nine.

5
00:00:14,259 --> 00:00:17,741
Each of them is a sum of two squares.

6
00:00:17,761 --> 00:00:20,465
Five is one plus four.

7
00:00:20,485 --> 00:00:23,538
Thirteen is four plus nine.

8
00:00:23,558 --> 00:00:27,018
Seventeen is one plus sixteen.

9
00:00:27,038 --> 00:00:30,647
And twenty-nine is four plus twenty-five.

10
00:00:30,667 --> 00:00:36,947
But three, seven, eleven and nineteen never are, no matter how hard you try.

11
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:39,897
What separates the two groups?

12
00:00:39,917 --> 00:00:42,080
Divide by four.

13
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,453
The first group leaves remainder one.

14
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:48,780
The second group leaves remainder three.

15
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:52,041
This is Fermat's two-square theorem.

16
00:00:52,061 --> 00:00:56,950
Today I want to show you a proof of it that fits in one sentence,

17
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:02,764
and a picture of windmills that explains why that sentence works.

18
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:09,472
First, the precise statement.

19
00:01:09,492 --> 00:01:12,094
Let p be an odd prime.

20
00:01:12,114 --> 00:01:17,788
Then p can be written as x² + y², with x and y integers,

21
00:01:17,808 --> 00:01:23,813
if and only if p is congruent to one, modulo four.

22
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:25,583
Geometrically,

23
00:01:25,603 --> 00:01:31,145
the circle x² + y² = p passes through points of the integer

24
00:01:31,165 --> 00:01:37,732
lattice exactly when p is one more than a multiple of four.

25
00:01:38,367 --> 00:01:41,370
The story begins with Fermat.

26
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:45,492
In a letter to Mersenne, dated Christmas Day, 1640,

27
00:01:45,512 --> 00:01:50,164
he stated this theorem and claimed he had a proof.

28
00:01:50,184 --> 00:01:53,378
As usual, he never wrote it down.

29
00:01:53,398 --> 00:01:58,329
That is why it is sometimes called Fermat's Christmas theorem.

30
00:01:58,667 --> 00:02:03,838
The first published proof came more than a century later, from Euler,

31
00:02:03,858 --> 00:02:07,485
using a method called infinite descent.

32
00:02:07,505 --> 00:02:13,668
Later came a deeper explanation, through what we now call the Gaussian integers,

33
00:02:13,688 --> 00:02:15,480
named after Gauss.

34
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:22,231
And in 1990, Don Zagier published a paper with a remarkable title.

35
00:02:22,251 --> 00:02:30,275
A one-sentence proof that every prime p congruent to one mod four is a sum of two squares.

36
00:02:30,295 --> 00:02:34,998
It builds on an earlier idea of Roger Heath-Brown.

37
00:02:35,018 --> 00:02:39,226
That one sentence is what we will unpack today.

38
00:02:42,433 --> 00:02:47,062
One direction is easy, and it is worth seeing first.

39
00:02:47,082 --> 00:02:53,088
Every square leaves remainder zero or one when divided by four.

40
00:02:53,108 --> 00:02:57,151
An even number squared is a multiple of four.

41
00:02:57,171 --> 00:03:01,979
An odd number squared is one more than a multiple of four.

42
00:03:01,999 --> 00:03:07,542
Look, the square of an odd number splits into one small square in the middle,

43
00:03:07,562 --> 00:03:10,246
plus four equal rectangles.

44
00:03:10,266 --> 00:03:13,356
Keep this little windmill in mind.

45
00:03:13,376 --> 00:03:15,379
We will meet it again.

46
00:03:15,399 --> 00:03:21,385
So a sum of two squares can only be zero, one or two, modulo four.

47
00:03:21,405 --> 00:03:23,316
Never three.

48
00:03:23,336 --> 00:03:28,336
That rules out every prime of the form 4k + 3.

49
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:30,976
But notice something.

50
00:03:30,996 --> 00:03:35,157
Twenty-one is also one more than a multiple of four,

51
00:03:35,177 --> 00:03:38,694
and yet it is not a sum of two squares.

52
00:03:38,714 --> 00:03:44,526
So the condition is necessary, but on its own it is not sufficient.

53
00:03:44,546 --> 00:03:48,216
Primality must matter somewhere in the proof.

54
00:03:48,236 --> 00:03:50,453
Keep this in mind.

55
00:03:50,473 --> 00:03:53,879
We will find exactly where it is used.

56
00:03:54,299 --> 00:03:58,738
The real content of the theorem is the other direction.

57
00:03:58,758 --> 00:04:04,495
Every prime that leaves remainder one really is a sum of two squares.

58
00:04:04,515 --> 00:04:09,527
And that is hard, because it is a pure existence statement.

59
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:14,306
There is no formula that simply hands you x and y.

60
00:04:17,399 --> 00:04:22,230
So how do you prove that something exists without finding it?

61
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:25,437
One classic answer is to count.

62
00:04:25,457 --> 00:04:31,626
If you can show that the number of solutions is odd, then it cannot be zero.

63
00:04:31,965 --> 00:04:37,135
And to show that a number is odd, you do not need to count exactly.

64
00:04:37,155 --> 00:04:40,005
You only need to pair things up.

65
00:04:40,332 --> 00:04:44,053
This leads to the key tool, an involution.

66
00:04:44,073 --> 00:04:49,688
An involution is a map from a set to itself that undoes itself.

67
00:04:49,708 --> 00:04:53,345
Apply it twice, and you are back where you started.

68
00:04:53,365 --> 00:04:58,217
An involution splits the set into pairs, a together with f of a,

69
00:04:58,237 --> 00:05:02,397
plus some points that are sent to themselves.

70
00:05:02,417 --> 00:05:04,745
Those are the fixed points.

71
00:05:04,765 --> 00:05:08,845
The pairs always contribute an even number.

72
00:05:08,865 --> 00:05:15,267
So the size of the set and the number of fixed points always have the same parity.

73
00:05:15,899 --> 00:05:21,706
Now suppose the same finite set carries two different involutions.

74
00:05:21,726 --> 00:05:26,010
Their numbers of fixed points have the same parity.

75
00:05:26,030 --> 00:05:30,491
In particular, if one of them has exactly one fixed point,

76
00:05:30,511 --> 00:05:36,064
the other has an odd number of fixed points, so it has at least one.

77
00:05:36,499 --> 00:05:39,443
If this sounds familiar, it should.

78
00:05:39,463 --> 00:05:44,545
Sperner's lemma is proved with the same kind of parity argument,

79
00:05:44,565 --> 00:05:47,279
by counting doors in pairs.

80
00:05:50,265 --> 00:05:52,629
So here is the plan.

81
00:05:52,649 --> 00:05:59,536
We want the solutions we are looking for to appear as the fixed points of an involution.

82
00:05:59,965 --> 00:06:04,640
Since p is odd, one of the two squares must be even.

83
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:13,664
So we are really looking for p equals x squared plus the square of two y,

84
00:06:13,684 --> 00:06:15,711
that is, x² + 4·y·y.

85
00:06:15,731 --> 00:06:18,665
Now comes the creative step.

86
00:06:18,685 --> 00:06:22,954
Replace one of the two y's by a new letter, z.

87
00:06:22,974 --> 00:06:29,980
Consider every triple of positive integers with x² + 4yz = p,

88
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,711
and call this set S.

89
00:06:32,731 --> 00:06:38,980
S is finite, because x squared and y z are both smaller than p.

90
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,211
For p equal to thirteen, for example, S has exactly three elements.

91
00:06:45,231 --> 00:06:48,766
This new set has an obvious symmetry.

92
00:06:48,786 --> 00:06:51,067
Swap y and z.

93
00:06:51,087 --> 00:06:53,040
That is an involution,

94
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:58,887
and its fixed points are exactly the triples with y equal to z.

95
00:06:58,907 --> 00:07:03,717
Then p equals x squared plus the square of two y.

96
00:07:03,737 --> 00:07:07,619
Those are precisely the solutions we want.

97
00:07:08,065 --> 00:07:12,210
So the whole theorem reduces to one question.

98
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:15,604
Is the number of elements of S odd?

99
00:07:15,624 --> 00:07:17,948
And by the parity principle,

100
00:07:17,968 --> 00:07:24,424
it is enough to find a second involution on S with exactly one fixed point.

101
00:07:24,765 --> 00:07:27,395
Notice what just happened.

102
00:07:27,415 --> 00:07:30,091
We did not find a symmetry.

103
00:07:30,111 --> 00:07:36,054
We created one, by splitting y times y into y times z.

104
00:07:36,074 --> 00:07:40,002
Making the problem bigger gave us room to move.

105
00:07:43,198 --> 00:07:45,804
To find the second involution,

106
00:07:45,824 --> 00:07:50,899
let us draw the equation. x squared is a square of side x.

107
00:07:50,919 --> 00:07:55,813
Four y z is four rectangles, each y by z.

108
00:07:55,833 --> 00:08:01,681
Attach one rectangle to each side of the square, all turning the same way,

109
00:08:01,701 --> 00:08:05,744
and you get a windmill whose area is exactly p.

110
00:08:05,764 --> 00:08:09,127
Every element of S is a windmill.

111
00:08:09,147 --> 00:08:13,211
Here are all eleven windmills of area forty-one.

112
00:08:13,231 --> 00:08:18,189
In this picture, swapping y and z simply flips each blade over,

113
00:08:18,209 --> 00:08:21,944
so that its length and width trade places.

114
00:08:21,964 --> 00:08:26,095
The fixed windmills are the ones with square blades.

115
00:08:26,115 --> 00:08:27,646
And look.

116
00:08:27,666 --> 00:08:34,025
Four squares of side y fit together into one square of side two y.

117
00:08:34,045 --> 00:08:39,403
So a fixed windmill is literally p, split into two squares.

118
00:08:42,498 --> 00:08:46,334
Now comes the most beautiful observation.

119
00:08:46,354 --> 00:08:51,694
Erase the lines inside a windmill, and keep only its outline.

120
00:08:51,714 --> 00:08:55,544
The outline does not remember how it was cut.

121
00:08:55,564 --> 00:09:00,619
The same outline can often be cut into a windmill in a different way,

122
00:09:00,639 --> 00:09:03,858
around a different central square.

123
00:09:04,198 --> 00:09:06,148
Here is the rule.

124
00:09:06,168 --> 00:09:11,045
Look at the four tips of the blades, and draw the square through them.

125
00:09:11,065 --> 00:09:15,646
If that square fits inside the outline, cut along it.

126
00:09:15,666 --> 00:09:17,037
If it does not fit,

127
00:09:17,057 --> 00:09:22,288
use the square through the outer edges of the blades instead.

128
00:09:22,731 --> 00:09:27,338
Sometimes the new square is larger, sometimes it is smaller,

129
00:09:27,358 --> 00:09:33,348
and sometimes the new windmill turns the other way, like a mirror image.

130
00:09:33,368 --> 00:09:38,797
Written in coordinates, this rule becomes Zagier's famous formula,

131
00:09:38,817 --> 00:09:40,578
with three cases.

132
00:09:40,598 --> 00:09:47,488
Cutting again brings back the original windmill, so re-cutting is an involution.

133
00:09:47,508 --> 00:09:52,707
A larger square becomes smaller again, a smaller one becomes larger,

134
00:09:52,727 --> 00:09:55,974
and the mirrored case mirrors back.

135
00:09:56,298 --> 00:10:00,329
When is a windmill unchanged by re-cutting?

136
00:10:00,349 --> 00:10:06,809
Exactly when the square through the tips is the central square itself, that is,

137
00:10:06,829 --> 00:10:08,625
when x equals y.

138
00:10:09,064 --> 00:10:12,521
And this is where primality enters.

139
00:10:12,541 --> 00:10:22,140
If x equals y, then p equals x squared plus four x z, which is x times (x + 4z).

140
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:25,639
Since p is prime, x must be one.

141
00:10:25,659 --> 00:10:31,430
So the only fixed windmill is the one with x and y both equal to one,

142
00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:34,632
a perfectly symmetric cross.

143
00:10:35,498 --> 00:10:37,694
One more detail.

144
00:10:37,714 --> 00:10:41,823
The rule never gets stuck on a boundary case.

145
00:10:41,843 --> 00:10:47,073
Those cases would need p to be a perfect square, or an even number,

146
00:10:47,093 --> 00:10:49,871
and an odd prime is neither.

147
00:10:50,198 --> 00:10:52,988
Now put everything together.

148
00:10:53,008 --> 00:10:57,754
The re-cutting involution has exactly one fixed point,

149
00:10:57,774 --> 00:11:01,205
so S has an odd number of elements.

150
00:11:01,225 --> 00:11:05,637
Therefore the flipping involution also has a fixed point,

151
00:11:05,657 --> 00:11:08,539
a windmill with square blades.

152
00:11:08,559 --> 00:11:12,710
And that is p, written as a sum of two squares.

153
00:11:13,231 --> 00:11:18,736
This is Zagier's entire proof, and it really is one sentence.

154
00:11:22,429 --> 00:11:26,609
When I first saw the formula, it looked like magic.

155
00:11:26,629 --> 00:11:32,607
But every step has a reason, and the reasons are the most valuable part.

156
00:11:32,929 --> 00:11:36,947
First, we needed existence, not a formula.

157
00:11:36,967 --> 00:11:42,842
Existence follows from an odd count, and an odd count follows from pairing.

158
00:11:42,862 --> 00:11:48,756
Second, to use pairing, the solutions we want must be fixed points.

159
00:11:48,776 --> 00:11:52,339
So we built a set with a built-in symmetry,

160
00:11:52,359 --> 00:11:55,942
by splitting y squared into y times z.

161
00:11:55,962 --> 00:12:02,503
Third, we needed a second involution, and the algebra alone gives no hint.

162
00:12:02,523 --> 00:12:04,553
The picture does.

163
00:12:04,573 --> 00:12:10,109
An area that can be cut in two ways is a natural source of pairs.

164
00:12:10,129 --> 00:12:14,306
Fourth, primality is used at exactly one sharp point,

165
00:12:14,326 --> 00:12:17,336
to make the fixed point unique.

166
00:12:17,356 --> 00:12:19,819
Test this with twenty-one.

167
00:12:19,839 --> 00:12:25,170
Its set S has two fixed windmills, the cross with x equal to one,

168
00:12:25,190 --> 00:12:28,972
and another one with x equal to three.

169
00:12:28,992 --> 00:12:32,942
Two is even, so the argument proves nothing.

170
00:12:32,962 --> 00:12:37,457
And indeed, twenty-one is not a sum of two squares.

171
00:12:37,895 --> 00:12:41,011
This is a good habit in general.

172
00:12:41,031 --> 00:12:45,569
For any proof, ask where exactly each hypothesis is used,

173
00:12:45,589 --> 00:12:48,320
and what breaks without it.

174
00:12:51,329 --> 00:12:54,012
There is one more surprise.

175
00:12:54,032 --> 00:12:58,198
The proof seems to say only that a solution exists,

176
00:12:58,218 --> 00:13:02,007
but it secretly contains an algorithm.

177
00:13:02,329 --> 00:13:07,198
Start from the cross, the unique fixed point of re-cutting.

178
00:13:07,218 --> 00:13:09,305
Flip the blades.

179
00:13:09,325 --> 00:13:10,993
Re-cut.

180
00:13:11,013 --> 00:13:12,719
Flip again.

181
00:13:12,739 --> 00:13:14,611
Re-cut again.

182
00:13:14,631 --> 00:13:16,184
Every step is forced,

183
00:13:16,204 --> 00:13:20,975
and we can never return to a windmill we have already visited.

184
00:13:20,995 --> 00:13:23,881
Since S is finite, the walk must stop,

185
00:13:23,901 --> 00:13:29,758
and it can only stop at a windmill that flipping leaves unchanged.

186
00:13:29,778 --> 00:13:35,035
For forty-one, after ten steps we reach blades of size two by two,

187
00:13:35,055 --> 00:13:40,933
and we read off that forty-one is five squared plus four squared.

188
00:13:41,262 --> 00:13:43,862
Why does this always work?

189
00:13:43,882 --> 00:13:49,473
Think of each windmill as a dot, and join partners with lines.

190
00:13:49,493 --> 00:13:53,327
Every dot has at most two lines, one of each kind,

191
00:13:53,347 --> 00:13:57,435
so the picture breaks into paths and loops.

192
00:13:57,455 --> 00:14:03,062
The cross is the end of a path, and the other end must be a solution.

193
00:14:03,495 --> 00:14:06,554
If you know the proof of Sperner's lemma,

194
00:14:06,574 --> 00:14:11,489
this is the same idea as walking through rooms and doors.

195
00:14:11,509 --> 00:14:14,870
It is not the fastest method, though.

196
00:14:14,890 --> 00:14:18,876
For large primes, there are much faster algorithms,

197
00:14:18,896 --> 00:14:22,098
based on the Euclidean algorithm.

198
00:14:25,495 --> 00:14:27,757
Now let us zoom out.

199
00:14:27,777 --> 00:14:32,912
Which whole numbers, not just primes, are sums of two squares?

200
00:14:33,361 --> 00:14:36,671
The key is a multiplication rule.

201
00:14:36,691 --> 00:14:42,600
The product of two sums of two squares is again a sum of two squares.

202
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:45,278
In the language of complex numbers,

203
00:14:45,298 --> 00:14:51,154
this just says that the length of a product is the product of the lengths.

204
00:14:51,595 --> 00:14:56,554
Combine this with Fermat's theorem, and you get the full answer.

205
00:14:56,574 --> 00:15:02,496
A positive integer is a sum of two squares exactly when every

206
00:15:02,516 --> 00:15:08,842
prime factor of the form 4k + 3 appears an even number of times.

207
00:15:09,261 --> 00:15:13,506
In the Gaussian integers, numbers of the form a plus b i,

208
00:15:13,526 --> 00:15:17,468
this becomes a statement about factoring.

209
00:15:17,488 --> 00:15:25,060
A prime that is one mod four splits into two conjugate factors,

210
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:28,384
like five equals (2 + i)(2 − i).

211
00:15:28,404 --> 00:15:32,600
A prime that is three mod four cannot be split.

212
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:34,696
It stays prime.

213
00:15:35,128 --> 00:15:39,002
Unique factorization in the Gaussian integers

214
00:15:39,022 --> 00:15:43,352
also tells us something Zagier's proof does not.

215
00:15:43,372 --> 00:15:50,508
The way of writing p as a sum of two squares is unique, up to order and signs.

216
00:15:50,928 --> 00:15:53,486
And the story continues.

217
00:15:53,506 --> 00:15:57,841
Which primes are x squared plus two y squared?

218
00:15:57,861 --> 00:16:02,105
Two, and the primes that are one or three mod eight.

219
00:16:02,125 --> 00:16:05,866
Which are x squared plus three y squared?

220
00:16:05,886 --> 00:16:09,652
Three, and the primes that are one mod three.

221
00:16:09,672 --> 00:16:13,887
Questions like these lead to quadratic reciprocity,

222
00:16:13,907 --> 00:16:17,449
and eventually to class field theory.

223
00:16:17,469 --> 00:16:21,036
And if you allow four squares instead of two,

224
00:16:21,056 --> 00:16:27,179
Lagrange proved that every positive integer is a sum of four squares.

225
00:16:30,195 --> 00:16:32,350
What is it good for?

226
00:16:32,370 --> 00:16:35,429
Here are two applications I like.

227
00:16:35,761 --> 00:16:39,412
The first is about right triangles.

228
00:16:39,432 --> 00:16:42,885
If p equals a squared plus b squared,

229
00:16:42,905 --> 00:16:49,918
then squaring a plus b i gives a squared minus b squared, plus two a b i.

230
00:16:49,938 --> 00:16:53,028
So a squared minus b squared, two a b,

231
00:16:53,048 --> 00:16:57,761
and p form a right triangle with integer sides.

232
00:16:57,781 --> 00:17:00,779
Five gives three, four, five.

233
00:17:00,799 --> 00:17:04,487
Thirteen gives five, twelve, thirteen.

234
00:17:04,507 --> 00:17:05,207
In fact,

235
00:17:05,227 --> 00:17:09,940
the odd primes that can be the hypotenuse of an integer

236
00:17:09,960 --> 00:17:15,604
right triangle are exactly the primes that are one mod four.

237
00:17:16,028 --> 00:17:20,042
The second is a surprise involving pi.

238
00:17:20,062 --> 00:17:25,840
Count the integer points inside a large circle of radius R.

239
00:17:25,860 --> 00:17:29,194
There are about pi R squared of them.

240
00:17:29,214 --> 00:17:34,151
There is a second way to count the same points, using divisors,

241
00:17:34,171 --> 00:17:38,590
and it rests on the theory of sums of two squares.

242
00:17:38,610 --> 00:17:43,054
Comparing the two counts gives Leibniz's formula.

243
00:17:43,074 --> 00:17:49,661
Pi over four equals one, minus one third, plus one fifth, minus one seventh,

244
00:17:49,681 --> 00:17:51,062
and so on.

245
00:17:51,495 --> 00:17:58,957
So a question about which primes are sums of two squares ends up connected to geometry,

246
00:17:58,977 --> 00:18:02,417
to complex numbers, and even to pi.

247
00:18:02,437 --> 00:18:08,119
That web of connections is a big part of why I love this theorem.

248
00:18:11,228 --> 00:18:13,662
Now let us practice.

249
00:18:13,682 --> 00:18:18,142
Here is how an interview about this theorem might go.

250
00:18:18,162 --> 00:18:21,701
Notice that each answer is short and precise,

251
00:18:21,721 --> 00:18:26,249
and leaves something the examiner can follow up on.

252
00:18:27,494 --> 00:18:30,508
Examiner: So, what is your favorite theorem?

253
00:18:30,528 --> 00:18:34,951
My favorite theorem is Fermat's two-square theorem.

254
00:18:34,971 --> 00:18:39,228
It says that an odd prime is a sum of two squares if

255
00:18:39,248 --> 00:18:44,680
and only if it leaves remainder one when divided by four.

256
00:18:44,700 --> 00:18:48,251
I like it because the statement is so simple,

257
00:18:48,271 --> 00:18:55,041
but the proof I like best is a clever counting argument that fits in one sentence.

258
00:18:55,061 --> 00:19:01,174
Examiner: Can you show me why primes of the form four k plus three can't work?

259
00:19:01,194 --> 00:19:02,529
Sure.

260
00:19:02,549 --> 00:19:06,375
Any square is zero or one, mod four.

261
00:19:06,395 --> 00:19:09,869
An even number squared is divisible by four,

262
00:19:09,889 --> 00:19:15,141
and an odd number squared is one more than a multiple of four.

263
00:19:15,161 --> 00:19:20,908
So a sum of two squares is zero, one or two, mod four, but never three.

264
00:19:20,928 --> 00:19:26,208
Examiner: Is that condition enough for any number, not just for primes?

265
00:19:26,228 --> 00:19:27,548
No.

266
00:19:27,568 --> 00:19:33,271
Twenty-one leaves remainder one, but it is not a sum of two squares.

267
00:19:33,291 --> 00:19:36,922
That's because twenty-one is three times seven,

268
00:19:36,942 --> 00:19:40,841
and both of those primes are three mod four.

269
00:19:40,861 --> 00:19:41,888
In general,

270
00:19:41,908 --> 00:19:47,751
a positive integer is a sum of two squares exactly when every prime

271
00:19:47,771 --> 00:19:53,941
factor of the form four k plus three appears an even number of times.

272
00:19:53,961 --> 00:19:55,325
Examiner: Good.

273
00:19:55,345 --> 00:19:58,741
Examiner: How would you prove the hard direction?

274
00:19:58,761 --> 00:20:01,365
I'd use Zagier's proof.

275
00:20:01,385 --> 00:20:09,022
Take the set S of positive triples with x squared plus four y z equal to p.

276
00:20:09,042 --> 00:20:12,184
Swapping y and z is an involution,

277
00:20:12,204 --> 00:20:18,605
and its fixed points give p as x squared plus the square of two y.

278
00:20:18,625 --> 00:20:23,582
So it's enough to show that S has an odd number of elements.

279
00:20:23,602 --> 00:20:26,452
For that, there's a second involution,

280
00:20:26,472 --> 00:20:30,062
which you can picture as re-cutting a windmill,

281
00:20:30,082 --> 00:20:33,297
and it has exactly one fixed point.

282
00:20:33,317 --> 00:20:38,341
By parity, S is odd, so the swap has a fixed point too.

283
00:20:38,361 --> 00:20:42,841
Examiner: Where exactly do you use the fact that p is prime?

284
00:20:42,861 --> 00:20:47,767
Mainly in one place, the uniqueness of the fixed point.

285
00:20:47,787 --> 00:20:54,636
A fixed windmill has x equal to y, so p equals x times x plus four z,

286
00:20:54,656 --> 00:20:58,752
and primality forces x to be one.

287
00:20:58,772 --> 00:21:05,750
It also rules out the boundary cases, which would need p to be a square or even.

288
00:21:05,770 --> 00:21:10,039
For twenty-one, for example, there are two fixed windmills,

289
00:21:10,059 --> 00:21:13,041
and the argument fails, as it should.

290
00:21:13,061 --> 00:21:15,036
Examiner: Interesting.

291
00:21:15,056 --> 00:21:20,074
Examiner: But how would anyone come up with that second involution?

292
00:21:20,094 --> 00:21:24,688
Honestly, from the algebra alone, I don't think I would.

293
00:21:24,708 --> 00:21:28,870
But if you draw x squared plus four y z as a windmill,

294
00:21:28,890 --> 00:21:34,498
you see that the same outline can be cut in two different ways.

295
00:21:34,518 --> 00:21:40,692
Whenever an object can be described in two ways, you get a natural pairing.

296
00:21:40,712 --> 00:21:47,008
So the geometry suggests the involution, and the formula just records it.

297
00:21:47,028 --> 00:21:50,802
Examiner: Your proof only shows existence.

298
00:21:50,822 --> 00:21:54,441
Examiner: Can you actually find x and y?

299
00:21:54,461 --> 00:21:58,797
Yes, the proof actually contains an algorithm.

300
00:21:58,817 --> 00:22:04,946
Start at the fixed point of re-cutting and alternate the two involutions.

301
00:22:04,966 --> 00:22:10,022
Each windmill has at most two neighbours, so the walk can't repeat,

302
00:22:10,042 --> 00:22:15,460
and it has to end at a fixed point of the swap, which is a solution.

303
00:22:15,480 --> 00:22:19,800
It's like the path-following proof of Sperner's lemma.

304
00:22:19,820 --> 00:22:22,336
It's slow for large primes, though,

305
00:22:22,356 --> 00:22:27,274
and there are faster methods using the Euclidean algorithm.

306
00:22:27,294 --> 00:22:29,974
Examiner: Do you know any other proof?

307
00:22:29,994 --> 00:22:34,002
Yes, there's one using the Gaussian integers.

308
00:22:34,022 --> 00:22:41,282
Since minus one is a square mod p, p divides n squared plus one for some n,

309
00:22:41,302 --> 00:22:46,225
and that factors as n plus i, times n minus i.

310
00:22:46,245 --> 00:22:49,545
If p stayed prime in the Gaussian integers,

311
00:22:49,565 --> 00:22:55,075
it would have to divide one of those factors, which is impossible.

312
00:22:55,095 --> 00:22:59,061
So p splits as a plus b i, times a minus b i,

313
00:22:59,081 --> 00:23:05,465
and taking norms gives p equals a squared plus b squared.

314
00:23:05,485 --> 00:23:09,196
There's also Euler's proof by infinite descent,

315
00:23:09,216 --> 00:23:12,574
and a pigeonhole argument due to Thue.

316
00:23:12,594 --> 00:23:16,841
Examiner: Why is minus one a square modulo p?

317
00:23:16,861 --> 00:23:23,456
By Wilson's theorem, p minus one, factorial, is minus one mod p.

318
00:23:23,476 --> 00:23:26,927
Pair each number k with minus k.

319
00:23:26,947 --> 00:23:32,476
When p is one mod four, the minus signs come in an even number,

320
00:23:32,496 --> 00:23:38,688
so the factorial of p minus one over two, squared, is minus one.

321
00:23:38,708 --> 00:23:41,541
Again, it's a pairing argument.

322
00:23:41,561 --> 00:23:44,708
Examiner: What about sums of three squares?

323
00:23:44,728 --> 00:23:49,112
That's harder, and I know the statement better than the proof.

324
00:23:49,132 --> 00:23:54,425
Legendre's three-square theorem says that a positive integer

325
00:23:54,445 --> 00:23:59,431
is a sum of three squares unless it has the form four to the a,

326
00:23:59,451 --> 00:24:02,126
times eight b plus seven.

327
00:24:02,146 --> 00:24:08,181
The easy half is again a congruence argument, this time mod eight.

328
00:24:08,201 --> 00:24:14,408
And with four squares, Lagrange proved that every positive integer works.

329
00:24:14,428 --> 00:24:18,841
Examiner: Is there an application you find meaningful?

330
00:24:18,861 --> 00:24:23,107
I like the connection to Pythagorean triples.

331
00:24:23,127 --> 00:24:29,138
Squaring a plus b i shows that the primes that can be the hypotenuse of

332
00:24:29,158 --> 00:24:35,823
an integer right triangle are exactly the primes that are one mod four.

333
00:24:35,843 --> 00:24:42,574
And counting lattice points in a circle even leads to Leibniz's series for pi.

334
00:24:42,594 --> 00:24:44,791
Examiner: Last question.

335
00:24:44,811 --> 00:24:47,874
Examiner: What did you learn from this theorem?

336
00:24:47,894 --> 00:24:49,707
Three things.

337
00:24:49,727 --> 00:24:56,448
First, you can prove existence by counting, sometimes just modulo two.

338
00:24:56,468 --> 00:25:02,119
Second, sometimes you have to create a symmetry, not just find one.

339
00:25:02,139 --> 00:25:08,762
And third, a good picture can turn a mysterious formula into something natural.

340
00:25:08,782 --> 00:25:12,341
I first met the parity idea in Sperner's lemma,

341
00:25:12,361 --> 00:25:17,333
and seeing it again here made me realize it's a general strategy,

342
00:25:17,353 --> 00:25:19,171
not just a trick.

343
00:25:22,117 --> 00:25:25,659
Let me end with what this theorem taught me.

344
00:25:25,983 --> 00:25:29,644
To prove that something exists, you can count it,

345
00:25:29,664 --> 00:25:33,691
and sometimes counting modulo two is enough.

346
00:25:33,711 --> 00:25:36,886
Symmetry is not always given.

347
00:25:36,906 --> 00:25:39,836
Sometimes you have to create it.

348
00:25:39,856 --> 00:25:43,350
And a good picture can turn a formula that looks like

349
00:25:43,370 --> 00:25:47,782
magic into something you could have discovered yourself.

350
00:25:48,217 --> 00:25:51,956
Brouwer's theorem is about fixed points.

351
00:25:51,976 --> 00:25:55,096
Sperner's lemma is about parity.

352
00:25:55,116 --> 00:25:59,972
And Zagier's proof is about the parity of fixed points.

353
00:25:59,992 --> 00:26:03,833
Ideas in mathematics keep meeting each other,

354
00:26:03,853 --> 00:26:10,095
and following those connections is, for me, the real joy of the subject.

355
00:26:10,850 --> 00:26:13,161
Thank you for watching.

